  "worked_solution": "First, let's draw our region $D$ and rectangle $R:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14149458/assets/56f6557f98674b0f651a781e6226ba9d207273e30136e3396c3aabcf91a64024.png)\n\nNotice that the rectangle $R$ contains the region $D$ completely. \n\nNow, if we define $f(x,y)$ to be equal ${2}^{x}-y$ on $D$ and to be equal $0$ outside $D,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}({2}^{x}-y)\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given functions.\n\n- We cannot use functions I and II. Both of these functions are not identically zero for all points in $R∖D.$ For example, $(x,y)=(1,0)\\in R∖D$, but the functions are nonzero at this point.\n\n- Function III equals ${2}^{x}-y$ on $D$ and equals $0$ outside $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
